] [17592186421713 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d8ae37cc-586d-5f42-83a1-41443720bcf7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421714 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421714 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421714 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421714 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421714 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421714 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421714 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421714 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186421633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421714 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d91d4103-d958-5264-a7e2-ea19b7cddb7c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421715 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421643 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421715 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421715 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421715 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421715 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421715 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421715 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421715 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186421692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421715 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fe48b365-00e3-52fe-9ee6-28adef726d37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421716 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421716 #edb/ref 1069 "q-77200" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421716 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421716 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $R,$ defined by $x∼y$ if ${x}^{3}={y}^{3}.$ Which of the following are in the equivalence class $[8]?$\n\nI. $-2$\n\nII. $2$\n\nIII. $8$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421716 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "071418af-96d7-52c9-8845-42c31b88cc12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421716 #edb/ref 1074 "The equivalence class $[8]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $8$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[8] & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,x∼8} \\\\  & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,{x}^{3}={8}^{3}} \\\\  & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,x=8} \\\\  & ={8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $8$ is in $[8],$ while $-2$ and $2$ are not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421717 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421717 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221718" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421717 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421717 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $Z,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $7\\,|\\,(x-y).$ Which of the following are in the equivalence class $[5]?$\n\nI. $12$\n\nII. $-4$\n\nIII. $-2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421717 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "22db406c-85ef-5a5a-ab22-d062645b4362" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421717 #edb/ref 1074 "The equivalence class $[5]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $5$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[5] & ={x\\in Z\\,:\\,x∼5} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,:\\,7\\,|\\,(x-5)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,:\\,x=5+7k,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={…,-9,-2,5,12,19,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $12$ and $-2$ are in $[5],$ while $-4$ is not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421718 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421718 #edb/ref 1069 "q-77205" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421718 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421718 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $C,$ defined by $x∼y$ if ${x}^{2}={y}^{2}.$ Which of the following are in the equivalence class $[-4]?$\n\nI. $-4$\n\nII. $4$\n\nIII. $4\\text{i}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421718 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3c1db003-08b1-562e-8980-51389652bb7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421718 #edb/ref 1074 "The equivalence class $[-4]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $-4$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[-4] & ={x\\in C\\,|\\,x∼-4} \\\\  & ={x\\in C\\,|\\,{x}^{2}=(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & ={x\\in C\\,|\\,{x}^{2}=16} \\\\  & ={-4,4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-4$ and $4$ are in $[-4],$ while $4\\text{i}$ is not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421719 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421719 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221767" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421719 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421719 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $R,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y.$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $3$\n\nI. $[0]$\n\nII. $[1]$\n\nIII. $[2]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421719 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "46852a07-1e77-54f8-9037-1f17a12c391f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421719 #edb/ref 1074 "We have $3\\in [x]$ if $3∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\cdot 3 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\pi  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x \\\\ -1 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $3\\notin [0]$ because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)=1\\ne -1\\,\\times $\n\n- $3\\in [1]$ because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)=-1\\,✓$\n\n- $3\\notin [2]$ because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 2)=1\\ne -1\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421720 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421720 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221800" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421720 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421720 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on the set $A={0,\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4},$ defined by $x∼y$ if ${x}^{4}={y}^{4}.$ How many distinct equivalence classes does $∼$ partition $A$ into?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421720 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "67dde538-2d6b-5e8f-8a86-3af3686c7b06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421720 #edb/ref 1074 "The equivalence classes of $∼$ partition $A,$ meaning that any element $a\\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={x\\in A∣x∼0} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}={0}^{4}} \\\\  & ={0} \\\\  \\\\ [1] & ={x\\in A∣x∼1} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}={1}^{4}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}=1} \\\\  & ={-1,1} \\\\  \\\\ [2] & ={x\\in A∣x∼2} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}={2}^{4}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}=16} \\\\  & ={-2,2} \\\\  \\\\ [3] & ={x\\in A∣x∼3} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}={3}^{4}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}=81} \\\\  & ={-3,3} \\\\  \\\\ [4] & ={x\\in A∣x∼4} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}={4}^{4}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}=256} \\\\  & ={-4,4} \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll of the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $5$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[0]={0},\\,[1]={-1,1},\\,[2]={-2,2},\\,[3]={-3,3},\\,[4]={-4,4}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the equivalence classes form a partition of $A,$ as expected." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421721 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421721 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221722" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421721 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421721 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $Z,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $11\\,|\\,(x-y).$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $-6?$\n\nI. $[5]$\n\nII. $[-17]$\n\nIII. $[-8]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421721 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d844129-f571-5c80-934e-cfc1faad53cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421721 #edb/ref 1074 "We have $-6\\in [x]$ if $-6∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11\\,|\\,(-6-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $-6\\in [5]$ because $11\\,|\\,(-6-5)\\,✓$\n\n- $-6\\in [-17]$ because $11\\,|\\,(-6+17)\\,✓$\n\n- $-6\\notin [-8]$ because $11∤(-6+8)\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421722 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421722 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221717" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421722 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421722 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $R,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Which of the following are in the equivalence class $[0]?$\n\nI. $2\\pi $\n\nII. $3\\pi $\n\nIII. $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421722 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a9e1ee25-17a0-56ec-9f69-bcad0ae7d0cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421722 #edb/ref 1074 "The equivalence class $[0]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $0$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,x∼0} \\\\  & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡0} \\\\  & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1} \\\\  & ={…,-4\\pi ,-2\\pi ,0,2\\pi ,4\\pi ,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $2\\pi $ is in $[0],$ while $3\\pi $ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421723 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421723 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86223" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421723 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421723 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $R,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $\\sqrt[3]{x}=\\sqrt[3]{y}.$ Which of the following are in the equivalence class $[-8]?$\n\nI. $-8$\n\nII. $8$\n\nIII. $2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421723 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bb353c96-604c-5c65-9adf-47096b9ec124" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421723 #edb/ref 1074 "The equivalence class $[-8]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $-8$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[-8] & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,x∼-8} \\\\  & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,\\sqrt[3]{x}=\\sqrt[3]{-8}} \\\\  & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,\\sqrt[3]{x}=-2} \\\\  & ={-8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-8$ is in $[-8],$ while $8$ and $2$ are not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421724 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421724 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86231" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421724 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421724 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $Z,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $2\\,|\\,(x-y).$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $9?$\n\nI. $[5]$\n\nII. $[2]$\n\nIII. $[19]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421724 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c88f13fc-8d9b-5fc7-ac75-4d5797158af8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421724 #edb/ref 1074 "We have $9\\in [x]$ if $9∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\,|\\,(9-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $9\\in [5]$ because $2\\,|\\,(9-5)\\,✓$\n\n- $9\\notin [2]$ because $2∤(9-2)\\,\\times $\n\n- $9\\in [19]$ because $2\\,|\\,(9-19)\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421725 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421725 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221801" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421725 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421725 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on the set $A={0,\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2},$ defined by $x∼y$ if $\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}=\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}}.$ How many distinct equivalence classes does $∼$ partition $A$ into?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421725 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cd31c5ed-d4ae-58a5-ab4f-af985ca907a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421725 #edb/ref 1074 "The equivalence classes of $∼$ partition $A,$ meaning that any element $a\\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={x\\in A∣x∼0} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}=\\sqrt[3]{{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}=0} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}=0} \\\\  & ={0} \\\\  \\\\ [1] & ={x\\in A∣x∼1} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}=\\sqrt[3]{{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}=1} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}=1} \\\\  & ={-1,1} \\\\  \\\\ [2] & ={x\\in A∣x∼2} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}=\\sqrt[3]{{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}=\\sqrt[3]{4}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}=4} \\\\  & ={-2,2} \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll of the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $3$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[0]={0},\\,[1]={-1,1},\\,[2]={-2,2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the equivalence classes form a partition of $A,$ as expected." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421726 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421726 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221762" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421726 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421726 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on the set $A={0,1,2,…,9},$ defined by $a∼b$ if $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi a)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi b).$ How many distinct equivalence classes does $∼$ partition $A$ into?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421726 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d047cc89-db39-59a7-988f-ed7cc91aeaba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421726 #edb/ref 1074 "The equivalence classes of $∼$ partition $A,$ meaning that any element $a\\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={a\\in A∣a∼0} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi a)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi a)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi a)=1} \\\\  & ={0,2,4,6,8} \\\\  \\\\ [1] & ={a\\in A∣a∼1} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi a)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi a)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi a)=-1} \\\\  & ={1,3,5,7,9} \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll of the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $2$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[0]={0,2,4,6,8},\\,[1]={1,3,5,7,9}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421727 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421727 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221743" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421727 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421727 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on the set $A={0,\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4},$ defined by $x∼y$ if ${x}^{2}={y}^{2}.$ How many distinct equivalence classes does $∼$ partition $A$ into?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421727 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d77f9f0f-9817-528d-99c4-631fe49372a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421727 #edb/ref 1074 "The equivalence classes of $∼$ partition $A,$ meaning that any element $a\\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={x\\in A∣x∼0} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}={0}^{2}} \\\\  & ={0} \\\\  \\\\ [1] & ={x\\in A∣x∼1} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}={1}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}=1} \\\\  & ={-1,1} \\\\  \\\\ [2] & ={x\\in A∣x∼2} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}={2}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}=4} \\\\  & ={-2,2} \\\\  \\\\ [3] & ={x\\in A∣x∼3} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}={3}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}=9} \\\\  & ={-3,3} \\\\  \\\\ [4] & ={x\\in A∣x∼4} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}={4}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}=16} \\\\  & ={-4,4} \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll of the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $5$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[0]={0},\\,[1]={-1,1},\\,[2]={-2,2},\\,[3]={-3,3},\\,[4]={-4,4}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the equivalence classes form a partition of $A,$ as expected." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421728 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421728 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221739" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421728 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421728 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on the set $A={0,1,2,…,20},$ defined by $a∼b$ if $5∣(b-a).$   How many distinct equivalence classes does $∼$ partition $A$ into?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421728 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f558440e-fbbe-5f40-9ac9-a9ba45c6c38a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421728 #edb/ref 1074 "The equivalence classes of $∼$ partition $A,$ meaning that any element $a\\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼0} \\\\  & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,5∣(a-0)} \\\\  & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,5∣a} \\\\  & ={0,5,10,15,20} \\\\  \\\\ [1] & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼1} \\\\  & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,5∣(a-1)} \\\\  & ={1,6,11,16} \\\\  \\\\ [2] & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼2} \\\\  & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,5∣(a-2)} \\\\  & ={2,7,12,17} \\\\  \\\\ [3] & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼3} \\\\  & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,5∣(a-3)} \\\\  & ={3,8,13,18} \\\\  \\\\ [4] & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼4} \\\\  & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,5∣(a-4)} \\\\  & ={4,9,14,19} \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll of the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $5$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[0]={0,5,10,15,20},\\,[1]={1,6,11,16},\\,[2]={2,7,12,17},\\,[3]={3,8,13,18},\\,[4]={4,9,14,19}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the equivalence classes form a partition of $A,$ as expected." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421729 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421729 #edb/ref 1069 "q-77214" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421729 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421729 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $Z,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $3\\,|\\,(x-y).$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $5?$\n\nI. $[3]$\n\nII. $[-1]$\n\nIII. $[8]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421729 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fd41a54f-8627-52be-9ff1-ad5548f86090" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421729 #edb/ref 1074 "We have $5\\in [x]$ if $5∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\,|\\,(5-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $5\\notin [3]$ because $3∤(5-3)\\,\\times $\n\n- $5\\in [-1]$ because $3\\,|\\,(5-(-1))\\,✓$\n\n- $5\\in [8]$ because $3\\,|\\,(5-8)\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
